 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>After a season spent indoors, Antonio Marras took his spring 2027 show into the open air, the most literal exit he could have staged. The invitation was a wedding announcement: that of Ippolita and Antiogu, in Alghero, “the midday after St. John’s night.” A long banquet table stood on the basketball court of Giardino Vincenzo Muccioli, a public park in Milan’s Tortona district, its asphalt covered in overlapping rugs. The audience sat a long while in the noon sun with the familiar impatience of guests waiting for the bride and groom. “A wedding is the moment when different people come together,” Marras said backstage. “This is the wedding of the day after, when we’re a little wrecked, a little tired, when everything is a bit undone, disheveled, unhinged.”</p><p>“Fashion also has a duty to unhinge things, to take things apart,” he continued. His own starting point is construction: “I start out as a minimalist, but I lose myself in the process. Then I need to encrust, dirty, embroider, leave the marks of our existence.” Checks and stripes collided with painterly flowers and reworked camouflage, a Marras staple. Military trousers came overlaid with sequined lace flowers; a navy jacket banded in fringe went over a lace skirt; a sprigged chiffon shirtdress was strapped into a boned corset; argyle knee socks turned up under tailored shorts. The last stretch went to black, down to a strapless ball gown with an embroidered bodice and lace veil.</p><p>Jewelry, a new collaboration with Giovanni Raspini, cast Sardinian masks, geckos, and flowers in burnished bronze. Overall, Sardinia was simply there, as it often is in Marras’s work. “We are the stratification of an existence,” he said. “Phoenicians, Romans, Greeks, French, Spanish—everyone arrived in Sardinia and left traces. I put my hands into that endless reservoir. It’s a DNA made of small fragments, the way my fashion is made of small details you discover as you go.” Even the collection’s title hid another layer: “A Midsummer Midday Wedding” nodded to Shakespeare, whose midsummer comedy ends with the wedding of Theseus and Hippolyta, Ippolita in Italian, while Antiogu is the Sardinian form of Antioco.</p><p>For the finale, the models returned in pairs, each carrying their own chair—on a shoulder, upside down, or held flat—before taking a place at the table. Then Edward Watson, former Royal Ballet principal, climbed onto it and revisited the banquet-table dance from Miloš Forman’s Hair, trading its exuberance for “Dio, come ti amo,” sung by Paola Turci with Paolo Fresu. The newlyweds came last: the bride in ecru tulle and a veil, the groom in an embroidered short suit and white socks.</p></div>",
